--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Monday, the 5th of January, 1931 (2/2 key facts kept) ---
I thought of trying to pawn my husband's watch again, to get coal for the fire, but his silver watch is already in pawn at Lamb's; I pawned Bert's Sunday suit for 5s. Od., Mrs. Annie Pollock came across with a few lumps of coal, seeing my fire was out, I read in the evening paper of the death of the Princess Royal, and I read of London being troubled with Princess Louise's increased weakness; Millwall beat Charlton by six goals to nil at football.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Monday, the 5th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
I read in the Daily Herald the announcement of the death of Princess Louise, the King's eldest sister, and I was sorry to hear of her passing. I also read that she had been seriously ill for some months, but that this was the first recent indication of a recurrence of the heart trouble from which she suffered. I saw Millwall beat Charlton by six goals to nil in a football match, and was glad to think of the popular amusement provided.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Monday, the 5th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
I read in The Times the death of Princess Louise. I read also that Princess Louise is showing increased weakness. The office was not cleaned this morning, and Mrs. Turner was given notice for not coming.
