--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Wednesday, the 7th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
I went round asking for work, but could not get any; I read in the evening paper that almost every day is inaugural day somewhere in the United States, recalling Democratic victories; I read that the big seaplane Do-X is scheduled to start her transatlantic flight to America on January 20; I read that the Prince of Wales and Prince George are to sail for South America on January 17; and I read that Lady Chamberlain and her son are going to the United States for a holiday.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Wednesday, the 7th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
1. I read in the Daily Herald:

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Wednesday, the 7th of January, 1931 (0/2 key facts kept) ---
I read in The Times of almost every day being inaugural day somewhere in the United States during the first weeks of the New Year, as new governors take the oath of office in 33 states, recalling Democratic victories at the elections in ... I read of the Dornier works announcing that the big seaplane Do-X is scheduled to start her transatlantic flight to America on January 20. And I read of the Prince of Wales and Prince George, his brother, sailing on January 17 from here to Santander to board the Oropesa, bound for a visit to the United States.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that she was cleaning an office in which there was a telephone, and a kind gentleman came in and spoke to her, and gave her work to do.
