--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Thursday, the 8th of January, 1931 (0/1 key facts kept) ---
I read in the evening paper that Britons were astonished at reports of widespread insubordination at the naval yard at Devonport, and that the sailing orders of the submarine depot ship Lucia had been countermanded.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Thursday, the 8th of January, 1931 (3/3 key facts kept) ---
To-day I spoke with Mr. Walter Thomas about the work in hand; I spoke with young Mr. Fenn about what the paper says: Britons, accustomed to iron-handed discipline aboard the ships of His Majesty’s navy, were astonished to-day at...; I entered a wrong figure in the audit books; I resolved never to make an entry in the books without first satisfying myself that it was correct; I entered the correct total in the audit books (4541); I prepared the audit books.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Thursday, the 8th of January, 1931 (1/1 key facts kept) ---
1. I told Mr. Hale that the auditors come on Monday.
